WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.093
Lesson three maps the agent frameworks and NVIDIA's NeMo Agent Toolkit.

2
00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:09.383
The exam asks which tool fits a need, not how to write it from memory.

3
00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:14.357
Picture a logistics company with three agent teams on three frameworks.

4
00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:20.419
Routing uses LangGraph, customer email uses CrewAI, and invoicing uses AutoGen.

5
00:00:21.219 --> 00:00:24.623
The finance chief asks which agent costs the most per task,

6
00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:26.203
and which is most accurate.

7
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:29.385
Every team measures differently, so nobody can answer.

8
00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:34.913
LangChain brings the broadest integrations, and LlamaIndex centres on retrieval.

9
00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:37.584
CrewAI declares role-based teams,

10
00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:42.511
while AutoGen and Semantic Kernel focus on multi-agent conversation.

11
00:00:43.311 --> 00:00:46.653
LangGraph models an agent as a graph over typed state.

12
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:50.495
Nodes update it, and conditional edges choose what runs next.

13
00:00:51.295 --> 00:00:53.997
A checkpointer saves the state after every node.

14
00:00:54.327 --> 00:00:56.331
So a run can resume after a crash,

15
00:00:56.381 --> 00:00:59.653
be replayed for debugging, or pause for human approval.

16
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:04.393
The step budget lives in the routing function, not in a hopeful prompt.

17
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:08.470
And an interrupt before tools makes human approval a built-in feature.

18
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:14.873
NeMo Agent Toolkit, formerly AgentIQ, is NVIDIA's framework-agnostic layer.

19
00:01:15.203 --> 00:01:17.249
It assumes you already have agents,

20
00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.681
and adds a consistent way to compose, profile, evaluate, and observe them.

21
00:01:22.481 --> 00:01:25.333
In the toolkit, everything is a typed function:

22
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:27.679
tools, model calls, even whole agents.

23
00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:31.458
So one team's entire agent can become a tool for another.

24
00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.964
Workflows are wired in YAML,

25
00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:37.477
so swapping a model is a config edit, not a code change.

26
00:01:37.807 --> 00:01:41.704
Built-in agent types include ReAct, ReWOO, and router agents.

27
00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:46.796
A profiler attributes tokens, latency, and cost to each step,

28
00:01:46.846 --> 00:01:50.070
and an evaluation harness runs datasets in CI.

29
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.638
OpenTelemetry traces flow to Phoenix, Langfuse, or any collector.

30
00:01:55.438 --> 00:01:59.288
It also speaks MCP both ways: it can use MCP servers,

31
00:01:59.338 --> 00:02:01.937
and expose its own functions as one.

32
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:06.804
Now all three logistics agents report cost and accuracy the same way.

33
00:02:07.604 --> 00:02:12.183
A plain loop over an OpenAI-compatible client is fine for a simple,

34
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.634
single-purpose agent.

35
00:02:13.964 --> 00:02:17.002
Reach for a framework when you need durable state,

36
00:02:17.052 --> 00:02:20.399
branching, human approval, or multi-agent coordination.

37
00:02:21.199 --> 00:02:21.897
On the exam,

38
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:24.825
agents in several frameworks with no consistent

39
00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:27.379
measurement points to NeMo Agent Toolkit.

40
00:02:27.709 --> 00:02:30.545
It composes frameworks rather than replacing them,

41
00:02:30.595 --> 00:02:32.565
so read on for the full comparison.
